---
import Base from '@/layouts/Base.astro';

const design = 'https://github.com/hakula139/assistant-bench/tree/main/docs/design';
---

<Base title="Methodology">
  <article class="max-w-prose space-y-6 leading-relaxed">
    <h1 class="text-[1.75rem] leading-tight font-semibold">Methodology</h1>

    <section class="space-y-2">
      <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold">Votes</h2>
      <p>
        Every system in a family answers the same cases. One rater sees two outputs side by side
        without knowing which systems wrote them, and picks the better side, a tie, or both bad.
        Sides are counterbalanced, and some pairs return later with the sides swapped to measure how
        consistent the rater is.
      </p>
      <p>
        An output that fails a family's gates, for example by being truncated or written in the
        wrong script, loses its pair without a vote. Two identical outputs tie without a vote.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="space-y-2">
      <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold">Scores</h2>
      <p>
        A Bradley-Terry model fits every vote in the family, counting ties and both-bad votes as
        half a win and absorbing any preference for the left pane in a separate term. Scores sit on
        an Elo-like scale with the family's reference system at 1,000.
      </p>
      <p>
        The 95% intervals come from bootstrap resamples drawn by case. A system's rank spans from 1
        plus the systems whose interval lies entirely above its own, to 1 plus the systems whose
        interval reaches above its lower bound. A system with fewer than 40 decisive votes is marked
        preliminary.
      </p>
      <p>
        When several systems can rank first, the leader is the one with the lower mean cost per
        output, then the lower median latency, and finally the higher score.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="space-y-2">
      <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold">Diagnostics</h2>
      <p>
        Each round reports how far its order can be trusted. Rater agreement is how often a
        side-swapped repeat matched its original vote, and below 70% the separations are weaker than
        the intervals suggest. Separated pairs is the share of system pairs whose intervals do not
        overlap, and split-half τ compares the orders fit to odd and even sessions.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="space-y-2">
      <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold">Categories, cost, and latency</h2>
      <p>
        Each case carries a category, and each category refits the scores on its own votes, so
        category intervals are wider. Cost per output comes from token counts and list prices, and
        latency is the time from request to complete response.
      </p>
    </section>

    <p class="text-muted-foreground">
      The repository's design notes on <a href={`${design}/ranking.md`}>ranking</a> and on each{' '}
      <a href={`${design}/families`}>family</a> cover each method in detail.
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>
